WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.987
Topic and policy classifiers: four families.

2
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:06.424
When a rule needs judgment (“is this about car rentals?

3
00:00:06.704 --> 00:00:09.093
”, “is this asking for help committing a crime?

4
00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:10.781
”), you need a classifier.

5
00:00:11.061 --> 00:00:17.163
There are four families, and choosing among them is a model-selection decision you should make with evidence.

6
00:00:17.443 --> 00:00:20.088
A small general model with structured output.

7
00:00:20.368 --> 00:00:26.917
You write the policy as categories with definitions and ask a cheap, fast model for a label from a fixed enum.

8
00:00:27.197 --> 00:00:44.243
Anthropic’s content moderation guide recommends this kind of setup with a small model for high volume, shows a risk-level variant (auto-block high risk, send medium risk to people), and notes that detailed category definitions catch cases a bare category list misses.

9
00:00:44.523 --> 00:00:49.259
Its strengths are flexibility (change the policy by editing text) and nuance.

10
00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:54.360
Its weakness is that it reads attacker-controlled text, so it can be manipulated too.

11
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.069
Open safety models.

12
00:00:56.349 --> 00:01:08.616
Meta’s Llama Guard 4 is a 12B model that classifies both prompts and responses against a 14-category hazard taxonomy based on MLCommons, and accepts text and images.

13
00:01:08.896 --> 00:01:18.731
Its model card reports, on Meta’s internal English test set for output filtering, 69 percent recall at an 11 percent false-positive rate.

14
00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:26.392
Read that carefully: that’s a capable classifier that still misses about a third of unsafe outputs on its own benchmark.

15
00:01:26.672 --> 00:01:35.739
Google’s ShieldGemma (2B, 9B, 27B) returns a probability of a policy violation for four harm types, in English.

16
00:01:36.019 --> 00:01:40.691
Both run on your own hardware, which matters when data can’t leave your environment.

17
00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:42.720
Hosted moderation endpoints.

18
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:51.491
OpenAI’s moderation endpoint ( omni-moderation-latest ) is free, takes text and images, and returns per-category scores.

19
00:01:51.771 --> 00:02:03.717
Its docs say to treat the scores as signals for your own policy rather than an automatic blocking decision, and warn that scores can shift when the model is upgraded, so thresholds need recalibrating.

20
00:02:03.997 --> 00:02:08.285
Gemini exposes adjustable safety filters per harm category.

21
00:02:08.565 --> 00:02:14.091
These are cheap to adopt and you don’t control them: categories are theirs, not your product’s policy.

22
00:02:14.371 --> 00:02:15.843
Prompt-attack detectors.

23
00:02:16.123 --> 00:02:21.627
Specialized models flag injection and jailbreak attempts rather than harmful content.

24
00:02:21.907 --> 00:02:36.307
Llama Prompt Guard 2 comes in 86M and 22M sizes, reads 512 tokens at a time, labels text benign or malicious, and its card warns that adaptive attacks designed to evade it can succeed.

25
00:02:36.587 --> 00:02:47.659
Azure’s Prompt Shields separates user prompt attacks from document attacks (instructions hidden in retrieved content), and notes it was trained and evaluated mainly on English.
